// @check
// @error `describe` needs `T: Showable`, which demands `toString()` — but `U` is unbounded. Add it to `U`'s bound
// @hint redundant type annotation: `toString` is inferred as `string` — remove it to lean on inference
// @hint redundant type annotation: `describe` is inferred as `string` — remove it to lean on inference
// @hint redundant type annotation: `twice` is inferred as `string` — remove it to lean on inference
// The `: string` / `: boolean` return annotations below are DECLARED rather than
// deleted: a fixture for a type feature should read as the documentation it is,
// and the checker calling them redundant is a fact about inference, not about
// the example. (`@hint` is how a corpus file says "yes, on purpose".)
// THE CHAINING HOLE, closed by SUBSUMPTION — and it was a clause-1 hole, measured.
//
// `twice<U>` is unbounded and relays its `y: U` into `describe<T: Showable>`. At
// that inner call there is no instantiation type: `U` is still a type variable. The
// tempting move is to DEFER, the way every other generic-body constraint in this
// checker defers to the pin. Deferring is wrong here and the witness says so —
// before this, `twice(2.0)` was `vl check`-clean invalid wasm, because the deferred
// bound had no validator to re-ask it and nothing else looks.
//
// The demand IS knowable at the inner call: `U`'s own bound either grants what
// `T`'s bound needs or it does not, and that is a static question. Deciding it here
// also puts the error on the declaration that is wrong instead of on a caller three
// modules away — which is how Rust, Haskell and C++20 concepts all decide it.
//
// The passing twin (`bound-chains-through-generic.vl`) is the same program with
// `<U: Showable>`.
type Circle = { r: f64 }
type Showable = { toString(): string }

function toString(self: Circle): string {
  if self.r > 0.0 { return "C" }
  "c"
}

function describe<T: Showable>(x: T): string { "<" + x.toString() + ">" }
function twice<U>(y: U): string { describe(y) + describe(y) }

print(twice({ r: 1.0 }))
print(twice(2.0))
